2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] Because promptBuffer is a raw UTF‑8 byte buffer but the code slices it using the decoded string
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] length (characters), multi‑byte Unicode characters leave leftover bytes. Those leftovers decode
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] into non‑empty/garbage text on the next Enter, so the "+" prompt doesn't register an empty line.
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] Root cause (in promptForge):
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] - line = decoder.decode(promptBuffer) gives a JS string (characters).
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] - then n = line.length and promptBuffer = promptBuffer.slice(n) slices bytes using character count,
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] not byte count.
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] - multi‑byte chars (e.g. emoji, thin spaces) break that math, leaving bytes behind.
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] Quick fixes:
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] - Clear the buffer on Enter instead of slicing by line length:
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini]   promptBuffer = new Uint8Array(0);
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] - Or compute actual consumed bytes by re‑encoding the decoded string and slicing by the encoded
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] byte length:
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini]   const consumedBytes = encoder.encode(line).length; promptBuffer =
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] promptBuffer.slice(consumedBytes);
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] Also fix backspace handling so it deletes a whole Unicode codepoint (decode, remove last code point
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] from the string, re‑encode to bytes, store that into promptBuffer) instead of removing a single
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] byte.
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] Any of those will stop leftover UTF‑8 bytes from making the prompt appear non‑empty after Unicode
2025-08-09 17:33:00 [gpt-5-mini] input.
