2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] Looks good — here are quick notes and suggestions for testing/fixing promptBuffer behavior.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] What I see
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - promptBuffer is a Uint8Array storing raw bytes of what the user types.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - You append bytes one-by-one and decode with decoder.decode when needed.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Backspace logic does promptBuffer = promptBuffer.slice(0, -1) which removes one byte, not one
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] Unicode codepoint. That will break for multibyte UTF-8 characters.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - On Enter you compute n = encoder.encode(line).length and do promptBuffer = promptBuffer.slice(n).
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] That assumes promptBuffer contains exactly those encoded bytes in order, which is fragile if any
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] escape sequences or multi-byte edits happened.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] Potential bugs to test
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Type non-ASCII characters (e.g., “é”, “€”, emoji). Backspace should remove the whole character;
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] current code removes one byte and may leave invalid UTF-8.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Paste multi-byte text quickly. Confirm promptBuffer decoding equals displayed string.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Use arrow/escape sequences (if any) — currently treated differently; ensure they don't corrupt
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] the buffer.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Repeated backspace + enter combos; confirm promptBuffer and displayed line stay in sync.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] Recommended approaches (high-level)
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Simpler: keep a string buffer for the logical line (e.g., promptString) and only convert to bytes
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] when writing to terminal. Update promptString for backspace/insert using Unicode-aware ops (slice
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] by codepoints). Then set promptBuffer = encoder.encode(promptString) whenever you need the raw
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] bytes.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - If you must keep raw bytes: always decode promptBuffer via TextDecoder.decode to get the
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] canonical string, apply Unicode-aware edits to the string, then re-encode into promptBuffer. That
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] avoids slicing raw bytes to remove a code point.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - For backspace, remove the last Unicode codepoint, not last byte.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - For paste or multi-byte input, aggregate bytes into a temporary Uint8Array then decode to string
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] to update canonical state.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] - Use TextEncoder.encode on the canonical string to compute n (byte length) rather than computing
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] encoder.encode(line).length from separate sources.
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] Quick tests to run
2025-08-09 17:38:02 [gpt-5-mini] 1. Type "hello" → backspace → expect "hell".
