2025-08-10 17:56:12 [stdin] skinniest space between unicode in terminal
2025-08-10 17:56:12 [simon@midnightblue] skinniest space between unicode in terminal
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] Short answer
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Smallest visible space: hair space U+200A.
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Smallest non-breaking narrow visible: narrow no‑break space U+202F.
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Truly zero width (invisible): zero‑width space U+200B (or U+200C/U+200D for joiner/non‑joiner),
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] and U+2060 (word joiner) to prevent breaks.
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] Notes
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Rendering varies by terminal emulator and font: some fonts/terminals render hair space visibly,
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] others barely at all (or treat it like a regular space).
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Zero‑width chars are invisible but can still affect cursor movement, selection, line breaks, or
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] string length in programs.
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - If you need "skinny but visible" use U+200A; if you need no visual gap but still semantic effect,
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] use U+200B or U+2060 depending on break behavior.
2025-08-10 17:56:19 [gpt-5-mini] - Always test in the specific terminal/font you target.
2025-08-10 17:56:58 [stdin] what group are the codepoints?
2025-08-10 17:56:58 [simon@midnightblue] what group are the codepoints?
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] They’re all in the Unicode “General Punctuation” block (U+2000–U+206F).
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] General categories:
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200A HAIR SPACE — Zs (Space_Separator)
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] - U+202F NARROW NO-BREAK SPACE — Zs (Space_Separator)
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200B ZERO WIDTH SPACE — Cf (Other_Format)
2025-08-10 17:57:07 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200C ZERO WIDTH NON-JOINER — Cf (Other_Format)
