2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — as you defined it, thinSpace is three code points, not a single thin space.
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] The sequence you used:
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200B ZERO WIDTH SPACE (​)
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200C ZERO WIDTH NON-JOINER (‌)
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200D ZERO WIDTH JOINER (‍)
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] Properties and implications:
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - They are zero-width (invisible) characters, so they don't produce a visible thin gap.
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - U+200D (ZWJ) can participate in emoji/ligature sequences and change rendering when adjacent to
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] emoji.
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - string.length will be 3 (each is a BMP code point). Many width/column-counting routines treat
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] them as zero width; some terminals/font combos may behave unexpectedly.
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] - If you want a visible thin gap, use U+2009 (THIN SPACE, ' ') or U+200A (HAIR SPACE) or U+202F
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] (NARROW NO-BREAK SPACE), e.g. '\u2009'.
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] If you want, I can show a tiny snippet to inspect code points or replace that sequence with a
2025-08-13 11:19:26 [gpt-5-mini] single thin space.
