2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — the plus sign (+) is a legal character in filenames on modern filesystems (Linux, macOS,
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] Windows / NTFS, FAT). Practical notes:
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Portability: OK across OSes, but some legacy/embedded systems or very old tools might be quirky.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Shells/CLI: + is not a shell metacharacter, so you don’t need to escape it normally. Still best
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] practice: quote filenames when passing to commands.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] - URLs and web forms: in query strings a + often decodes as a space. In URL path segments it’s
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] literal, but you may want to percent‑encode (+ → %2B) for safety.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Tools and patterns:
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini]   - Regexes, globs and some programmatic APIs treat + as a special token — escape or quote when
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] used in patterns.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini]   - Some tooling that parses filenames (CSV, logs, query parameters, legacy APIs) may mis-handle +.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Recommendations:
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini]   - Use + freely for local files, but quote/escape when invoking commands and percent‑encode if
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] embedding in URLs.
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini]   - For maximum compatibility, prefer letters, digits, hyphen and underscore (a-zA-Z0-9_-).
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] If you tell me the exact environment (web upload, Git repo, cross-platform share, URLs, etc.) I can
2025-08-13 16:09:57 [gpt-5-mini] give a tailored recommendation.
2025-08-13 16:15:32 [stdin] js string to number
2025-08-13 16:15:32 [simon@midnightblue] js string to number
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] Short list — pick one:
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Unary plus: +str  — quick, trims whitespace, returns NaN on non‑numeric.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Number(str)  — like +, explicit.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - parseInt(str, 10)  — integer parsing, stops at first non‑digit; always pass radix.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - parseFloat(str)  — parse floating point, stops at first non‑numeric char.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - str * 1 or str / 1  — coerces to number (same as +).
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - BigInt(str)  — for very large integers (throws on decimals).
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Math.floor/Math.round/Math.trunc(Number(str))  — convert to integer with rounding/truncation.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] Edge cases:
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Number("") === 0 but parseInt("") → NaN.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Use Number.isNaN(x) or Number.isFinite(x) to validate conversions.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] - Locale: "1,234" is not parsed as 1234 by Number/parseFloat (comma is invalid); replace commas
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] first if needed.
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] Recommendation: for general numeric conversion use Number(str) or +str; for integer parsing use
2025-08-13 16:15:53 [gpt-5-mini] parseInt(str, 10); validate with Number.isFinite(Number(str)).
