2025-08-19 15:30:10 [simon@midnightblue] can ansi report cursor position?
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Yes, ANSI escape sequences can report the cursor position using the Device Status Report
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] (DSR) sequence:
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Query Cursor Position
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Send:
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] printf "\033[6n"
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Response (read from stdin):
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] \033[<row>;<column>R
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - `\033[` = CSI (Control Sequence Introducer)
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - `<row>` = Current row (1-based)
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - `<column>` = Current column (1-based)
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - `R` = Terminates the response
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Example in Shell (Bash)
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat]  Enable raw input to read the response
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] stty raw -echo
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat]  Send query
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] printf "\033[6n"
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat]  Read response (timeout after 1 second)
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] read -rs -t 1 -d R pos
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] stty sane
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat]  Extract row and column
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] [[ $pos =~ ^.*\[([0-9]+);([0-9]+)$ ]]
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] row=${BASH_REMATCH[1]}
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] col=${BASH_REMATCH[2]}
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] echo "Cursor at row=$row, column=$col"
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] Notes
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - Works in most terminal emulators (xterm, iTerm2, GNOME Terminal, etc.).
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - Some minimal environments (like `dumb` terminals) may not support it.
2025-08-19 15:30:30 [deepseek-chat] - If you're scripting, ensure the terminal is interactive (`[ -t 1 ]`).
