2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] The problem is in this section:
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] } else if (byte === 0x1b) { // Escape sequence
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat]     if (value.length === 1) {
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat]         return null;  // This only works for single-byte ESC
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat]     }
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat]     // ... rest of CSI handling
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] The issue is that when ESC is pressed alone, it correctly returns `null` to exit, but the logic
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] assumes `value.length === 1` for a standalone ESC key. However, in the async race condition with
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] the timer, the ESC key might be read as part of a larger buffer, making `value.length !== 1` even
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] for a single ESC press.
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] The escape handling should check if the first byte is ESC (0x1b) and then properly parse the
2025-08-21 16:27:53 [deepseek-chat] sequence rather than relying on buffer length alone.
